Yes — based on the excerpts provided, damage caused by a creek overflowing due to heavy rain would generally fall within the policy's definition of **Flood**, which is defined as "the covering of normally dry land by water that has escaped or been released from the normal confines of: any lake, or any river, creek or other natural watercourse, whether or not altered or modified; or any reservoir, canal or dam" [RAA Contents PDS, page 87].

The policy provides cover under Section 12, Flood, for "loss or damage to the Insured Property provide[d] as a result of Flood" [RAA Contents PDS, page 50].

However, this cover is subject to specific exclusions listed on page 50, including (among others):
- loss or damage to any part of the Home that is not fully built;
- loss or damage caused by underground (hydrostatic) water;
- loss or damage to paint where there is no structural damage caused by the Flood to other parts of the Home;
- loss or damage to unsealed paths or driveways;
- loss or damage to items such as paths or driveways that were not structurally sound or properly maintained prior to the loss

[RAA Contents PDS, page 50].

I don't have enough information in the excerpts to confirm whether any of these specific exclusions would apply to your situation, nor whether the "Good Condition" requirement [RAA Contents PDS, page 88] operates as a precondition to Flood cover, as the excerpts don't explicitly link that definition to a coverage condition. If your claim involves any of the excluded items or if there are questions about the property's condition prior to the flood, you may need to consult additional policy sections not included here.
